            <ArticleTitle>A Copy Number Variant on Chromosome 20q13.3 Implicated in Thinness and Severe Obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>623431</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2015/623431</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify copy number variants (CNVs) which are associated with body mass index (BMI).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS/METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">CNVs were identified using array comparative genomic hybridization (aCGH) on members of pedigrees ascertained through severely obese (BMI ≥ 35 kg/m(2)) sib pairs (86 pedigrees) and thin (BMI ≤ 23 kg/m(2)) probands (3 pedigrees). Association was inferred through pleiotropy of BMI with CNV log⁡2 intensity ratio.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 77-kilobase CNV on chromosome 20q13.3, confirmed by real-time qPCR, exhibited deletions in the obese subjects and duplications in the thin subjects (P = 2.2 × 10(-6)). Further support for the presence of a deletion derived from inference by likelihood analysis of null alleles for SNPs residing in the region.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">One or more of 7 genes residing in a chromosome 20q13.3 CNV region appears to influence BMI. The strongest candidate is ARFRP1, which affects glucose metabolism in mice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
